Idempotency lives at the controller boundary; the payment processor underneath stays unaware.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class PaymentController {
        +POST createPayment(req, idemKey)
        +GET getPaymentStatus(id)
    }
    class IdempotencyMiddleware {
        +execute(req, key, action) Response
    }
    class IdempotencyStore {
        <<interface>>
        +tryReserve(apiKey, key, fingerprint) ReservationResult
        +complete(apiKey, key, response)
        +lookup(apiKey, key) Optional~Stored~
    }
    class RedisIdempotencyStore
    class PostgresIdempotencyStore
    class TwoLayerIdempotencyStore
    class IdempotencyLock {
        +acquire(key, ttl) boolean
        +release(key)
    }
    class RequestFingerprinter {
        +fingerprint(body) String
    }
    class PaymentService {
        +process(PaymentRequest) PaymentResult
    }
    class OutboxWriter {
        +enqueue(payment) within tx
    }
    class GatewayClient {
        +charge(req, idemKey) GatewayResponse
    }

    PaymentController --> IdempotencyMiddleware
    IdempotencyMiddleware --> IdempotencyStore
    IdempotencyMiddleware --> IdempotencyLock
    IdempotencyMiddleware --> RequestFingerprinter
    IdempotencyMiddleware --> PaymentService
    IdempotencyStore <|.. RedisIdempotencyStore
    IdempotencyStore <|.. PostgresIdempotencyStore
    IdempotencyStore <|.. TwoLayerIdempotencyStore
    PaymentService --> OutboxWriter
    OutboxWorker --> GatewayClient
```

**Key code paths** (interesting bodies only):

```java
public class IdempotencyMiddleware {
    public Response execute(Request req, String apiKey, String key,
                            Function<Request, Response> action) {
        String hash = sha256(apiKey);
        String fp = fingerprinter.fingerprint(req.body());

        Optional<Stored> cached = store.lookup(hash, key);
        if (cached.isPresent()) return validateAndReplay(cached.get(), fp);

        if (!lock.acquire("lock:" + hash + ":" + key, Duration.ofSeconds(60))) {
            return waitForInflight(hash, key, fp);
        }
        try {
            ReservationResult r = store.tryReserve(hash, key, fp);
            if (r.isReplay()) return validateAndReplay(r.stored(), fp);

            Response resp = action.apply(req);
            store.complete(hash, key, resp);
            return resp;
        } finally {
            lock.release("lock:" + hash + ":" + key);
        }
    }

    private Response validateAndReplay(Stored s, String requestFp) {
        if (!s.fingerprint().equals(requestFp)) {
            return Response.conflict("idempotency_key_in_use");
        }
        return s.response().withHeader("Idempotent-Replayed", "true");
    }
}

public class TwoLayerIdempotencyStore implements IdempotencyStore {
    @Override public ReservationResult tryReserve(String hash, String key, String fp) {
        try {
            postgres.insertReservation(hash, key, fp);
            return ReservationResult.fresh();
        } catch (UniqueViolationException e) {
            Stored existing = postgres.find(hash, key);
            return ReservationResult.replay(existing);
        }
    }

    @Override public void complete(String hash, String key, Response r) {
        postgres.updateResponse(hash, key, r);
        redis.set("idem:" + hash + ":" + key,
                  serialize(r), Duration.ofHours(24));
    }
}
```

Why this shape:

- **Middleware wraps the action** — dedup is orthogonal to payment logic; one place enforces it for every endpoint that opts in.
- **`IdempotencyStore` interface** — the two-layer impl (Redis + Postgres) is one option; we could swap to DynamoDB for multi-region, or pure-Postgres for simpler deployment.
- **`RequestFingerprinter` extracted** — canonicalization rules evolve; one tested class.
- **`OutboxWriter` happens inside the same DB transaction as the payment row** — atomic guarantee that if the payment is recorded, the gateway will eventually be called.

**Choreography vs Orchestration** — with 7 services, implicit event-chain choreography becomes unmaintainable. We pick **orchestration**.

**Why orchestration wins here:**

- The flow is **readable as one method** — a reviewer or operator can see all 7 steps and their compensations in one file.
- **Debuggability** — a stuck order has one place to look (saga state).
- **Testability** — inject fakes for each service, exercise every compensation path with normal unit tests.
- **Adding a step** (e.g., "check tax service") is a one-place change.

**Orchestrator pseudocode:**

```java
public OrderResult placeOrder(OrderRequest req) {
    String orderId = orderService.create(req);                // status=CREATED
    saveSagaState(orderId, "CREATED");

    try {
        inventoryService.reserve(orderId, req.items(), Duration.ofMinutes(30));
        saveSagaState(orderId, "INVENTORY_RESERVED");
    } catch (InsufficientStockException e) {
        orderService.cancel(orderId, "out_of_stock");
        return OrderResult.failed("out_of_stock");
    }

    if (fraudService.score(req).isHighRisk()) {
        inventoryService.release(orderId);                    // compensate
        orderService.cancel(orderId, "fraud_block");
        return OrderResult.failed("fraud_review");
    }

    try {
        paymentService.charge(orderId, req.paymentMethod(), req.totalCents());
        saveSagaState(orderId, "PAYMENT_CONFIRMED");
    } catch (PaymentDeclinedException e) {
        inventoryService.release(orderId);                    // compensate
        orderService.cancel(orderId, "payment_declined");
        return OrderResult.failed("payment_declined");
    }

    fulfillmentService.assignWarehouse(orderId);              // these don't fail user-visibly;
    shippingService.createLabel(orderId);                      //   they enqueue work
    notificationService.sendConfirmation(orderId);

    saveSagaState(orderId, "COMPLETED");
    return OrderResult.success(orderId);
}
```

**Crash resilience** — saga state is persisted after each step. If the orchestrator crashes between steps, on restart it reads the saga state and **resumes from the next step** rather than replaying the whole thing. Resumption requires each downstream service call to be **idempotent** (we pass `order_id` as the idempotency key).

**Long-running steps (3D Secure)** — payment may take 30 seconds. The Saga becomes **suspended**: persists state as `WAITING_FOR_PAYMENT`, returns `202 Accepted` to client, resumes when the payment webhook arrives.

Order service is the orchestrator's home; cross-service calls are via interfaces.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class OrderController {
        +POST createOrder(req)
        +POST cancelOrder(id)
        +GET getOrder(id)
    }
    class OrderService {
        +create(req) OrderId
        +transition(id, event) Result
        +cancel(id, reason)
    }
    class OrderStateMachine {
        +canTransition(from, to) boolean
        +nextStateFor(event) State
    }
    class OrderRepository {
        <<interface>>
        +save(order)
        +loadById(id) Order
        +appendEvent(orderId, event)
    }
    class PostgresOrderRepository
    class OrderSagaOrchestrator {
        +place(req) OrderResult
        +resume(sagaId)
    }
    class SagaStateStore {
        +save(sagaId, state)
        +load(sagaId) SagaState
    }
    class InventoryClient {
        <<interface>>
        +reserve(orderId, items, ttl)
        +release(orderId)
        +confirm(orderId)
    }
    class PaymentClient {
        <<interface>>
        +charge(orderId, method, amount)
        +refund(orderId)
    }
    class FulfillmentClient
    class ShippingClient
    class NotificationClient
    class FraudClient
    class OutboxWriter

    OrderController --> OrderService
    OrderController --> OrderSagaOrchestrator
    OrderService --> OrderStateMachine
    OrderService --> OrderRepository
    OrderService --> OutboxWriter
    OrderRepository <|.. PostgresOrderRepository
    OrderSagaOrchestrator --> SagaStateStore
    OrderSagaOrchestrator --> InventoryClient
    OrderSagaOrchestrator --> FraudClient
    OrderSagaOrchestrator --> PaymentClient
    OrderSagaOrchestrator --> FulfillmentClient
    OrderSagaOrchestrator --> ShippingClient
    OrderSagaOrchestrator --> NotificationClient
```

**Key code paths** (interesting bodies only):

```java
public class OrderService {
    public Result transition(OrderId id, Event event) {
        Order order = repo.loadById(id);
        State next = stateMachine.nextStateFor(order.status(), event);
        if (next == null) return Result.illegal("no_transition_for_" + event);

        int updated = repo.updateStatus(id, order.version(), next);
        if (updated == 0) {                           // optimistic lock conflict
            return Result.retry();
        }
        repo.appendEvent(id, OrderEvent.of(event, next));
        outbox.write(new OrderStatusChanged(id, next));
        return Result.ok(next);
    }
}

public class OrderSagaOrchestrator {
    public OrderResult place(OrderRequest req) {
        OrderId id = orderService.create(req);
        sagaStore.save(id, SagaState.CREATED);
        try {
            inventory.reserve(id, req.items(), Duration.ofMinutes(30));
            sagaStore.save(id, SagaState.INVENTORY_RESERVED);
        } catch (InsufficientStockException e) {
            return cancelOrder(id, "out_of_stock");
        }
        if (fraud.score(req).isHighRisk()) {
            return compensateAndCancel(id, "fraud_block", List.of(this::releaseInventory));
        }
        try {
            payment.charge(id, req.paymentMethod(), req.totalCents());
            sagaStore.save(id, SagaState.PAYMENT_CONFIRMED);
        } catch (PaymentDeclinedException e) {
            return compensateAndCancel(id, "payment_declined", List.of(this::releaseInventory));
        }
        fulfillment.assignWarehouse(id);
        shipping.createLabel(id);
        notifications.sendConfirmation(id);
        sagaStore.save(id, SagaState.COMPLETED);
        return OrderResult.success(id);
    }

    public void resume(SagaId sagaId) {
        SagaState state = sagaStore.load(sagaId);
        switch (state.currentStep()) {
            case CREATED -> { /* re-attempt inventory reserve */ }
            case INVENTORY_RESERVED -> { /* re-attempt fraud + payment */ }
            case PAYMENT_CONFIRMED -> { /* re-attempt fulfillment + shipping */ }
            case COMPLETED -> { /* nothing */ }
        }
    }
}
```

Why this shape:

- **`OrderStateMachine` extracted** — the legal-transition table is one tested class; UI and API both consult it ("can this order be cancelled?").
- **Each downstream service has its own client interface** — lets us mock for tests, swap implementations, add retry/circuit-breaker per integration.
- **`SagaStateStore` is its own collaborator** — saga survival across orchestrator restarts is the saga pattern's whole point; can't be implicit.
- **Idempotency at every downstream call** — we pass `order_id` as the natural idempotency key. A resume after crash retries with the same key; downstream services return prior result instead of double-acting.
